Agentic AI — Interview Questions
& Prep Sheet
Covers: LLM Fundamentals → AI Agents → Agentic AI → MCP Servers
→ GitHub Copilot (Ask/Plan/Agent modes, Custom Agents) → Claude
Code (Skills & Subagents)
Section 1: LLM Fundamentals (Foundation)
1. What is a Large Language Model (LLM)? How does it
generate a response? An LLM is a neural network trained on
massive text corpora to predict the next token in a sequence.
Given a prompt, it generates text on learned probability
distributions —The base LLM itself predicts tokens and does not
independently access tools, persistent memory, or external
systems. An application or agent runtime can provide these
capabilities around the model.
2. What are the core limitations of a plain LLM (without any
agent framework around it)? No persistent memory across
sessions, no access to real-time/external data, no ability to take
actions (run code, call APIs, browse), and it can hallucinate
confidently. It only produces text — it doesn't do anything.
3. What is the difference between a chatbot and an LLM-based
application? A chatbot is one narrow use case (conversational
Q&A) built on an LLM. An LLM application can extend far beyond
chat — code generation, agents, RAG pipelines, orchestration
systems, etc.
Section 2: AI Agents — What & Why
4. What is an AI agent? How is it different from a plain LLM call?
An AI agent is an LLM combined with the ability to reason, plan,
use tools, take actions, observe results, and iterate toward a goal
— autonomously, with minimal step-by-step human instruction. A
plain LLM call just returns text for a single prompt; an agent
operates in a loop (perceive → plan → act → observe → repeat).
5. What are tokens, and how do they affect cost, latency, and
context-window usage?
Tokens are the units processed by an LLM. Larger prompts,
conversation histories, and tool results consume more context, increase
cost, and may increase latency.
6. What is "context window" and why does it matter for agentic
systems? It's the maximum amount of text (tokens) the model
can process at once, including prompt, history, and tool outputs.
Agent design (subagents, skills) exists partly to manage context
— offloading work so the main session's window doesn't get
flooded with irrelevant detail.
7. What are the key components of an AI agent architecture?
LLM (reasoning core), tools/functions it can call, memory (shortterm/context and sometimes long-term/persistent) and an
environment it acts in (filesystem, APIs, browser, etc.).
8. What does "tool use" or "function calling" mean in the
context of AI agents? The model's ability to recognize when it
needs external capability (search, code execution, database
query) and call a defined function/tool with structured parameters,
then use the returned result to continue reasoning.
9. Give a real-world example of an AI agent vs. a non-agentic
LLM use case. Non-agentic: asking ChatGPT "summarize this
paragraph." Agentic: "go find our Q3 sales numbers, compare
them to Q2, and draft a report" — where the system decides
which tools to use, fetches data, computes, and produces output
without being told each step.
Section 3: Agentic AI (The Bigger Picture)
10. What is "Agentic AI"? How is it different from a single AI
agent? Agentic AI refers to systems where one or more agents
autonomously plan, decide, and coordinate — often with multiple
specialized agents working together (orchestration) rather than
one agent doing everything. It's the broader paradigm; "an agent"
can be one building block within it.
11. What is agent orchestration, and why is it needed?
Orchestration is when a main/coordinator agent decomposes a
complex goal into subtasks and delegates them to specialized
subagents, then combines their results. It's needed because a
single agent handling everything in one context leads to context
overload, slower iteration, and less reliable results on complex,
multi-part tasks.
12. What distinguishes "agentic" behavior from just following
instructions step by step? Agentic behavior means the system
decides how to break down and solve a goal — which tools/skills/
subagents to invoke and in what order — versus a human
explicitly scripting every step. The hallmark is autonomous
planning and delegation.
13. What are common risks or challenges with agentic AI
systems? Model answer: Compounding errors across multi-step
chains, tool/permission misuse, cost and latency from multiple
agent calls, difficulty debugging emergent behavior, and the need
for guardrails (scoped tool access, validation steps) to keep
autonomy safe.
Section 4: MCP Servers (Model Context
Protocol)
14. What is an MCP server, and what problem does it solve? MCP
(Model Context Protocol) is a standard protocol that lets an AI
model/agent connect to external tools and data sources
(databases, APIs, file systems, SaaS apps) in a consistent way. It
solves the "every integration needs custom glue code" problem by
giving agents a uniform way to discover and call external
capabilities.
15. Give an example of when you'd use an MCP server in an
agent workflow. Connecting an agent to Playwright MCP (for
Browser Automation), Jira (to pull tickets), or Google Drive (to
read docs) — the agent calls the MCP server's exposed tools
instead of you writing custom API code for each.
16. How do MCP servers relate to "tools" that an agent can call?
MCP servers expose a set of tools/resources that get registered
as callable tools within the agent's toolset — so from the model's
perspective, an MCP-provided tool looks just like any other tool it
can invoke.
17. What security risks arise when connecting an agent to an
MCP server?
Risks include malicious tool descriptions, excessive permissions, data
leakage, prompt injection, unauthorized actions, and compromised
servers. MCP guidance explicitly treats tool-related metadata as
untrusted unless it comes from a trusted serve
Section 5: GitHub Copilot — Modes &
Custom Agents
18. What is the difference between "Ask," "Plan," and "Agent"
mode in GitHub Copilot? Model answer:
• Ask mode: conversational Q&A — you ask, it answers/
suggests, but doesn't take autonomous action on your
codebase.
• Plan/Planning mode: helps research and propose a
design/approach before execution — useful for scoping a
solution before code changes happen.
• Agent mode: autonomously decides which files to edit, runs
terminal commands, and iterates on errors with minimal
manual intervention — it actually executes the work.
19. When would you use Planning mode instead of jumping
straight into Agent mode? Model answer: When the task is
ambiguous, architecturally significant, or needs a reviewed
approach first — Planning mode lets you validate the "how" before
autonomous execution changes real files.
20. What is a "custom agent" in GitHub Copilot, and how do you
define one? Model answer: A custom agent is a lightweight,
scoped agent definition with its own name, system prompt, and
restricted toolset (and optional MCP servers), created so Copilot
can delegate matching requests to it automatically as a sub-agent
running in isolated context.
21. How does Copilot decide which custom agent to delegate a
task to? The Copilot runtime matches the incoming request
against each custom agent's defined expertise/description and
automatically routes to the best-fit agent — similar to how Claude
Code auto-invokes matching skills.
Section 6: Claude Code — Skills System &
Subagents
22. What is a "Skill" in Claude Code? What does a SKILL.md file
contain? A Skill is reusable procedural knowledge — a SKILL.md
file (name, description/trigger condition) plus instructions Claude
follows when the skill is relevant. It's knowledge Claude loads into
context, not a separate running process.
23. What is a Subagent in Claude Code, and how is it different
from a Skill? Model answer: A Subagent is a separate, isolated
Claude instance with its own context window that the main agent
delegates a specific task to; it runs independently and returns only
a summary. A Skill is passive know-how that gets loaded/invoked
— by the main agent or by a subagent — it doesn't run its own
isolated session. Skills = knowledge; Subagents = isolated
execution.
24. If you give Claude Code one high-level prompt and it
automatically invokes multiple skill files/subagents to solve
the problem — what concept does that demonstrate? That's
agentic AI in action: you gave a goal, not step-by-step instructions,
and the system autonomously planned, decided which skills/
subagents to invoke, and combined the results — the defining trait
of agentic behavior vs. simple instruction-following.
25. Why would you choose a Subagent/SubSkills over just
adding more instructions to the main session's system
prompt? Model answer: To avoid flooding the main context with
logs/search results/file contents that aren't needed long-term, to
get parallelism on independent subtasks, and to keep permission/
tool scope tightly restricted per task.
Section 7: Scenario / Applied Questions
26. You need an agent system that reviews a pull request against
your team's coding standards, using data from GitHub.
Design it — which pieces (Skill, Subagent, MCP) would you
use and why? MCP server (GitHub MCP) for PR diff/comment
access; a Skill encoding the team's review checklist (security,
naming, test coverage); a Subagent ("pr-reviewer") with read-only
tools that calls the MCP server, loads the skill for methodology,
and returns a summary to the main session.
